{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Where is Sarah your wife.</b> Rashi explained: to make known that she was modest, in order to endear her to her husband. But this is difficult: did Abraham not know until now that she was modest? And it appears to me that since He wanted to say ״I will return to you״ (Genesis 18:10), the implication is — but not to her, and one might suspect that on this account He would cast hatred upon her, saying that she was the cause for which HaShem had withheld from them the fruit of the womb. Therefore the angel saw fit to correct this, and said ״Where is Sarah your wife״ — even though the angel knew where she was, none the less he said: Do you not acknowledge that she is always in the tent because she is modest? And if so, surely the Holy One, blessed be He, will not withhold from her the reward of her modesty — for our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Megillah 10b): ״Every bride who is modest merits, and kings emerge from her, and a proof is from Tamar,״ etc. And she too was modest; therefore her merit brought about the promise ״and kings shall come forth from you״ (Genesis 17:6), ״and kings of peoples shall come from her״ (Genesis 17:16). And through this He endears her to her husband, for the merit of her modesty brought it about for her. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 48:15) that a point is placed over the אי״ו, because they asked also concerning Sarah ״אַיּוֹ״. And the darshanim of scriptural hints said (Baal ha-Turim) that ״אֵלָיו אַיֵּה״ are the letters of ״אֵלֶיהָ אַיּוֹ״. And upon this it is certainly difficult: how did they ask concerning Sarah ״אַיּוֹ״, when Abraham was standing over them?" "Therefore my heart tells me that the question ״אַיֵּה״ and ״אַיּוֹ״ is not a question of place — meaning, where is he or where is she — but rather a question of spiritual rungs, for the term ״place״ (מָקוֹם) denotes the spiritual rung. And they did not ask at all in what place he or she was, but rather they asked about the place of their rung and their excellence in the matter of their good deeds, on account of which they would both merit a son — for she merited a son because she was modest, and he merited a son through the merit of charity and hospitality. And just as it is hinted in these two words ״אֵלָיו אַיֵּה״ — as if he said ״אֵלֶיהָ אַיּוֹ״ — so too it is hinted in these two words: ״אִשְׁתְּךָ וַיֹּאמֶר״ becomes ״אִישֵׁךְ וַתֹּאמֶר״. And so it is as though it were written: ״And they said to her, Where is your husband, and she said, Here in the tent״ — for she too answered the question about the excellence of his rung: ״Here in the tent,״ for this is the rung of Abraham — to sit at the entrance of the tent and look out for guests, and he is always occupied with planting inn-tents for guests, as it says (Genesis 21:33) ״And he planted an eshel״ — that is, an inn (Sotah 10a), as it is written (Daniel 11:45) ״And he shall plant the tents of his palace״. And if so, the plain sense of the verse includes two matters: for its plain sense, ״And they said to him, Where is Sarah your wife״ — what is the place of the rung of her excellence, on account of which she will merit a son; and he answered ״Here in the tent״ — that through the merit of her being modest she will merit that kings shall come forth from her. And its midrashic sense: ״And they said to her, Where is your husband, and she said, Here in the tent״ — for the question ״Where is he״ is: at what rung is he, that he should merit a son? And she answered ״Here in the tent״ — that he is always planting inn-tents for guests, such that the merit of charity should stand for him to beget a son, as it is written (Psalms 17:14–15) ״They are satisfied with sons and leave their abundance to their infants; I — in righteousness, I shall behold Your face.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:47.618012118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}